---
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: traefik
  namespace: {{ k8s_namespace }}
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: traefik
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
  ingress:
{# THE ROLLBACK LEVER. All seven slice-4 policies carry this same conditional, and this file
   carries the reasoning the other six point back to.

   `netpol_baseline_enforced` is the documented undo for slice 4, and it only works if each
   bespoke policy renders allow-all when the flag is false. `kubectl apply` never prunes, so a
   `when:` on the include in tasks/main.yml would undo nothing: the policies already applied
   stay live and keep fencing, minus the traefik/prometheus grants the flag also switches off --
   strictly worse than not rolling back at all. Real deletion is not available either;
   `kubectl delete networkpolicy` is deny-listed for agents and refused by the readonly SA.
   Slice 3 established this render-allow-all pattern for the observability policies
   (networkpolicy-observability.yaml.j2); slice 4 follows it.

   `| bool` matches the `when:` in tasks/main.yml. Without it the two disagree for a STRING:
   `-e netpol_baseline_enforced=false` is truthy to Jinja (this branch renders, fencing
   applied) and False to Ansible's `when:` (every probe task skipped) -- enforcement with no
   probe, from the exact override an operator reaches for mid-incident. #}
{% if netpol_baseline_enforced | bool %}
    # The front door. Open to every source on purpose: these ports already take LAN, WireGuard
    # and Cloudflare traffic, and ETP=Local preserves the client IP, so an ipBlock list here
    # would have to enumerate the internet. :80 in particular must stay open for the
    # http->https redirect entrypoint (static-config.yaml.j2, entryPoint.to: ":443") -- every
    # client that arrives on plain HTTP hits that redirect before reaching :443. It also keeps
    # all four netpol probe control legs working -- each dials traefik:80 to prove the probe
    # itself has a network. (uptime-kuma's monitors do NOT depend on :80: they're https:// and
    # so traverse :443, or for the Pi services dial daniel-pi directly, bypassing traefik.)
    #
    # THE PORT NUMBERS HERE ARE POD PORTS, NOT SERVICE PORTS. A NetworkPolicy matches the
    # destination port on the POD, after kube-proxy has already DNAT'd the Service port to the
    # targetPort. traefik's Service maps 80 -> targetPort `http` and 443 -> `https`, and those
    # container ports are 8000 and 8443. Writing 80/443 here therefore admits traffic to two
    # ports nothing listens on, and denies every in-cluster caller of the real ones.
    # Measured 2026-08-20: that is exactly what happened -- the slice-1 probe's control leg
    # failed with "cannot reach traefik:80" while the same route answered 200 from the LAN,
    # because external traffic arrives over the VIP path rather than the ClusterIP.
    # Every other service in this slice maps its ports 1:1, which is why only this file was hit.
    - ports:
        - port: 8000
          protocol: TCP
        - port: 8443
          protocol: TCP
    # :8080 is the port worth fencing. prometheus moved to the observability namespace in
    # slice 3, so this is cross-namespace: namespaceSelector and podSelector are siblings in
    # ONE from item, which ANDs them. As separate items they would OR, admitting every pod in
    # observability and every prometheus anywhere.
    - from:
        - namespaceSelector:
            matchLabels:
              kubernetes.io/metadata.name: observability
          podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: prometheus
      ports:
        - port: 8080
          protocol: TCP
{% else %}
    # OFF. One empty rule admits everything. `ingress: []` would be the exact opposite and deny
    # everything, which is the easiest thing in this file to get backwards.
    - {}
{% endif %}
